  "question": "How much of M2's F1 24-config grid result is attributable to the hyperparameters and how much to the seed, and is the naive one-way test on seed values misleading?",
  "read_only": true,
  "reading": "The seed explains 78.8% of the variance in per-seed validation RankIC and the hyperparameters 17.2%, but those two numbers are not competing for the same question. The seed swing is a level shift common to every config, so it inflates the apparent noise of any single measurement without making configs harder to compare. Blocked on seed, the config effect is decisive (p~2e-9) and the learning-rate and hidden-dim margins are real. What is NOT resolvable is the top of the ranking: the SE of a config mean is 0.00133 while the rank1-to-rank2 gap is 0.00123, so the winner is picked from a tie at the top and only separates from roughly rank 5 downwards.",
